      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 25162.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 581,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (What it looks and feels like)\nUnder normal conditions on Earth, water is a liquid. It is unique because it is:\n* **Transparent:** Pure water is colorless, odorless, and tasteless, allowing light to pass through it (which is vital for aquatic plant life).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why things like salt, sugar, and vitamins easily dissolve in it. Because of this, it acts as a carrier for nutrients and chemicals in nature and inside our bodies.\n* **Present in Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical forms:\n  * **Liquid** (oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. Ecological Importance (Water and Earth)\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth’s surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle—evaporating from the oceans into the atmosphere, condensing into clouds, falling back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flowing back into the oceans via rivers. \n* **Climate Regulation:** Oceans absorb and store massive amounts of heat from the sun, which helps regulate global temperatures and weather patterns.\n\n### 3. Biological Importance (Water and Life)\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It is essential for survival; it regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and transports nutrients through the bloodstream. A human can survive weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n* **Habitat:** Water provides a home for millions of species of plants, animals, and microorganisms, ranging from microscopic plankton to the blue whale.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet. It shapes landscapes, drives the weather, makes up the majority of living creatures, and is the fundamental medium in which the chemistry of life takes place."
